WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.459 --> 00:00:02.819
hey it's professor dave, let's talk sig figs

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:12.400
humans have a need to communicate
their experiences with one another

00:00:12.410 --> 00:00:16.129
how far apart are two cities? when did
something happen

00:00:16.129 --> 00:00:19.420
and how long before it happens again? this is
why we developed

00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:23.170
units of measurement. some correlate with
natural phenomena

00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:26.580
like a day or year which are based on
the motion of the earth

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:30.310
but many are arbitrary like most units
of length and mass.

00:00:30.310 --> 00:00:34.570
when we participate in measurement we
have to be aware of some limitations

00:00:34.570 --> 00:00:38.780
for example how many people are in this
crowd? can we say

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:44.210
10,000? what about 10,400 or 10,472

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:48.180
we have to be careful not to make an
estimate that is more precise

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:52.010
than our measuring abilities allow so
when we use measuring devices

00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:55.760
we only estimate one digit further than
the level of precision

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:59.210
inherent in the instrument. here we can estimate

00:00:59.210 --> 00:01:03.989
2.33 centimeters but not 2.33481

00:01:03.989 --> 00:01:06.990
we do not have access to that level of precision

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:10.000
so any measurement will have a certain amount

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:13.330
of significant figures or sig figs

00:01:13.330 --> 00:01:16.600
associated with it that display how
precise it is

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:20.439
here are the rules for determining how
many sig figs are in a value

00:01:20.439 --> 00:01:23.640
one: any digit that's not zero

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:25.619
is significant

00:01:25.619 --> 00:01:29.040
two: zeros in between other non-zero digits

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:31.100
are significant

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:35.710
three: leading zeros, ones that come before
the first non-zero digit

00:01:35.710 --> 00:01:38.030
are not significant

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:40.880
and four: trailing zeros, the ones that come

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:46.750
after the last non-zero digit are only
significant if they are decimal zeros

00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:51.160
so 10,000 only has one significant
figure which really means it's about

00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:56.030
10,000 not exactly ten thousand. if we
bring back that same crowd and one more

00:01:56.030 --> 00:01:56.990
person shows up

00:01:56.990 --> 00:02:02.070
can we estimate ten thousand and one? no,
because we have only estimated to one sig fig

00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:06.100
so anything from 9,500 to 10,499

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:10.060
would round to our estimate of 10,000. when we do calculations we have to pay

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:11.930
attention to the number of sig figs

00:02:11.930 --> 00:02:16.850
if we are adding or subtracting our
answer will have as many decimal places

00:02:16.850 --> 00:02:21.879
as the value with the least number of
decimal places

00:02:21.879 --> 00:02:25.549
if we are multiplying or dividing our
answer will have as many sig figs

00:02:25.549 --> 00:02:30.510
as the value with the least number of sig figs

00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:33.660
whatever our answer is we'll have to
round up or down

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:37.220
at the appropriate digit. 5 or higher
means round up

00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:40.980
four or below means round down. let's check
comprehension

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:14.450
thanks for watching guys subscribe to my
channel for more tutorials

00:03:14.450 --> 00:03:16.510
and as always feel free to email me

